      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to determine the remainder when \\(n + 2010\\) is divided by 6, given that \\(n\\) leaves a remainder of 1 when divided by 6.\n\n1. **Understanding the Congruence Condition:**\n   Since \\(n\\) leaves a remainder of 1 when divided by 6, we can express this as:\n   \\[\n   n \\equiv 1 \\pmod{6}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Expressing \\(n + 2010\\) in Terms of Congruence:**\n   To find the remainder when \\(n + 2010\\) is divided by 6, we substitute \\(n \\equiv 1 \\pmod{6}\\) into the expression \\(n + 2010\\):\n   \\[\n   n + 2010 \\equiv 1 + 2010 \\pmod{6}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Simplifying the Expression Inside the Congruence:**\n   First, compute \\(1 + 2010\\):\n   \\[\n   1 + 2010 = 2011\n   \\]\n\n4. **Finding the Remainder of 2011 When Divided by 6:**\n   Now, we need to find the remainder of 2011 when divided by 6. We do this by performing the division:\n   \\[\n   2011 \\div 6\n   \\]\n   Calculate the quotient and the remainder:\n   \\[\n   2011 \\div 6 = 335 \\text{ remainder } 1\n   \\]\n   This can be verified by:\n   \\[\n   2011 = 6 \\times 335 + 1\n   \\]\n\n5. **Conclusion:**\n   Therefore, the remainder when \\(2011\\) is divided by 6 is 1. Hence, the remainder when \\(n + 2010\\) is divided by 6 is also 1.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{1}\n\\]",
